A||||7||artherd||Fire Extinguishers||||3||
Z||000000||artherd||08-25-2001||02:04 PM||||From another thread:<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>wouod it be completely out of line if we carried fire extinguishers?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Heck no!  I have not fewer than three extinguishers in every fiero (and any other car) I own...   One in the front compartment, one in the people compartment, and one in the boot/trunk.<P>It is not possible to have too many extinguishers, in case anyone ever forgets, internal combustion motor vehicles are just that, *combustion* under extreeme pressures at that, exlposions.<P>Anyone who dosen't carry an extinguisher in their car (any car) is in my opinion, rather a fool.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000001||theogre||08-25-2001||02:39 PM||||I have pretty much always had some type of extinguisher in all my cars.<P>First Alert makes (Made?) a small one. Its rated 2 B & C and is real small. It's made for kitchen use where for putting out a grease fire on/in a stove. This would make a good one for inside a car. It is big enough to put out allot of early stage car fires. It would buy you time to get to a big one stored else where.<P>For larger ones, 5-10 B & C rating is good for most car stuff.<P>I would not store one in the rear of a fiero. Reason being it that if there's a fire and you can't get to it. (Fire is more likely in rear.) the thing could go off like a bomb when it over heats. (They are suposed to safety vent but you can't always count on the valve working.)<P>A rate... wood & paper<BR>B rate... liquid & grease<BR>C rate... electrical<P>Dry chemical usually has low or no A rating. the little extinguisher I mentioned above has no A rate. Larger dry chem usually has an A rate of 1/3 to 1/2 the BC rate. So a 5 BC dry chem would have a 2-2.5 A rate.<P>For cars Dry Chemical is usually best. It will quash anything but burning vapor and is lighter than CO2.<P>I wish I could still get Halon units. You could shoot them thru any opening you could find w/o raising the hood etc. Great for a wreck where you might not be able to open the hood.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||08-25-2001||03:34 PM||||Remember: When your car starts to burn out of control, open the windows so that the fire can breath. That way, Insurance can't prove what condition the car was in before the fire, and with arbitration, has to give you the maximum value...<P>Sorry, did I say that out loud?||||reg||1
